        <h1>How to Compress Images for Website Speed (2026 Guide)</h1>

        <p class="lead">
          Slow images kill website performance. Uncompressed images are the #1 cause of slow page loads. Here's a step-by-step guide to compressing images for maximum speed without sacrificing quality.
        </p>

        <h2>Why Image Compression Matters for SEO</h2>
        <p>Google's Core Web Vitals directly measure how fast your page loads, and images are usually the heaviest assets on any page. A single uncompressed hero image can add 2-3 seconds to your load time. Here's what slow images cost you:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Higher bounce rates</strong> — 53% of mobile users abandon sites that take over 3 seconds to load</li>
          <li><strong>Lower search rankings</strong> — Google uses page speed as a ranking factor since 2018</li>
          <li><strong>Poor Core Web Vitals</strong> — Largest Contentful Paint (LCP) is directly affected by image load time</li>
          <li><strong>Higher hosting costs</strong> — Bandwidth isn't free; smaller images mean lower server bills</li>
          <li><strong>Worse mobile experience</strong> — Mobile networks are slower; every KB matters</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Image Compression Techniques</h2>

        <h3>Lossy Compression</h3>
        <p>Lossy compression removes some image data that humans can't easily perceive. JPG and WebP both use lossy compression. The quality parameter controls how much data is removed. At quality 85, the file is 40-60% smaller than the original with no visible difference for most photos.</p>

        <h3>Lossless Compression</h3>
        <p>Lossless compression reduces file size without removing any image data. PNG and lossless WebP use this method. File size reduction is typically 10-30%, smaller than lossy compression, but quality is preserved perfectly.</p>

        <h3>Responsive Images</h3>
        <p>Serving different image sizes for different screen sizes is one of the most effective optimization techniques. A mobile user doesn't need a 4K hero image. Use the <code>srcset</code> attribute to serve appropriately sized images:</p>

        <h2>Step-by-Step Image Optimization Workflow</h2>

        <h3>Step 1: Choose the Right Format</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Photographs</strong> → JPG or lossy WebP</li>
          <li><strong>Graphics with transparency</strong> → PNG or WebP</li>
          <li><strong>Icons and logos</strong> → SVG (if possible) or PNG</li>
          <li><strong>Animations</strong> → WebP or GIF (WebP is much smaller)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Step 2: Resize to the Display Size</h3>
        <p>Never serve a 4000px wide image in a 1200px container. Resize images to the maximum display size they'll actually be shown at. Common breakpoints:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Mobile: 640-768px wide</li>
          <li>Tablet: 1024px wide</li>
          <li>Desktop: 1280-1920px wide</li>
          <li>Hero images: 1920px wide is usually sufficient</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Step 3: Compress to the Optimal Quality</h3>
        <p>Use our <a href="/jpg-compressor">JPG compressor</a> to reduce file size while maintaining visual quality. For most photos, quality 80-85 is the sweet spot. Use our <a href="/image-compressor">image compressor</a> for batch processing multiple files at once.</p>

        <h3>Step 4: Convert to WebP</h3>
        <p>After compressing, convert to WebP for even smaller files. Our <a href="/jpg-to-webp">JPG to WebP converter</a> handles this in your browser with no upload required.</p>

        <h3>Step 5: Serve Responsive Images</h3>
        <p>Use HTML <code>srcset</code> to serve different sizes based on the user's screen. This ensures mobile users get small, fast-loading images while desktop users get the full resolution.</p>

